;\">My creator, Victor Frankenstein, recounts his life from childhood in Geneva, shaped by a loving family and a deep fascination with natural philosophy. Raised alongside his beloved foster sister Elizabeth Lavenza and his devoted friend Henry Clerval, he was drawn to the forbidden knowledge of alchemy after a storm revealed the power of electricity, leading him to abandon outdated theories for modern science. At the University of Ingolstadt, he became obsessed with the secret of life, ultimately constructing a monstrous creature from assembled body parts in a secluded workshop. Upon seeing his creation come to life, he was overcome with horror and fled, only to be reunited with Clerval, who nursed him through a severe illness after the creature vanished.\n   632→\n   633→The tragedy deepens when Victor returns to Geneva and learns of his younger brother William’s murder. Haunted by a vision of the creature at the crime scene, he is devastated to see Justine Moritz falsely accused and executed, knowing the truth but unable to speak it. Consumed by guilt, he seeks solace in nature, only to be confronted by the creature on the icy slopes of Mont Blanc. The creature, pleading for understanding, recounts his lonely existence—rejected by society, tormented by his appearance, and driven to violence by abandonment. Moved by his suffering, Victor agrees to create a female companion, but ultimately destroys the second creature in fear of unleashing a race of monsters. The creature vows revenge, threatening to be with Victor on his wedding night.\n   634→\n   635→Victor marries Elizabeth in a state of dread, misinterpreting the creature’s threat as aimed at himself, only to witness Elizabeth’s murder in their bridal chamber. Overwhelmed by grief, he vows to pursue the creature across the world, enduring years of relentless travel through Europe, Tartary, and Russia. His pursuit leads him to the Arctic, where he is rescued by Robert Walton’s ship after drifting on a melting ice floe. There, he recounts his entire tragic tale, warning Walton of the creature’s intelligence and danger. On his deathbed, Victor justifies his refusal to create a mate and implores Walton to destroy the creature, who appears at the coffin, confessing his own torment and murders before vanishing into the frozen sea, ending his life in a final act of self-destruction.</div>\n   636→                \n   637→                <p style=\"margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted);\">\n   638→                    <strong>Themes:</strong> identity, ambition, loss\n   639→                </p>\n   640→                \n   641→            </section>\n   642→            \n   643→\n   644→            \n   645→            <section>\n   646→                <h2>🤖 Analysis Models</h2>\n   647→                <table style=\"width: 100%; margin-top: 1rem; border-collapse: collapse;\">\n   648→                    <thead>\n   649→                        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid var(--primary);\">\n   650→                            <th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem; color: var(--muted);\">Phase</th>\n   651→                            <th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem; color: var(--muted);\">Model</th>\n   652→                            <th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem; color: var(--muted);\">Provider</th>\n   653→                        </tr>\n   654→                    </thead>\n   655→                    <tbody>\n   656→                        \n   657→                        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid var(--primary);\">\n   658→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem;\">Chapter Detection</td>\n   659→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem; font-family: monospace;\">qwen3:30b-instruct</td>\n   660→                            <td style=\"padding: 0.75rem;\">ollama</td>\n   661→                        </tr>\n   662→                        \n   663→                        <tr style=\"border-b