{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018gQ38m376RgfJHzzR3ZaEo","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"title\": \"Contents\",\n    \"author\": \"Mary Wollstonecraft (Godwin) Shelley\",\n    \"source_file\": \"Test_Texts/Frankenstein_ebook.txt\",\n    \"source_format\": \"txt\",\n    \"total_word_count\": 75060,\n    \"total_character_count\": 418299,\n    \"estimated_total_duration_minutes\": 499.2266666666667,\n    \"words_per_minute\": 150\n  },\n  \"structure\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 1,\n      \"start_position\": 1000,\n      \"end_position\": 31434,\n      \"word_count\": 5441,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 36.27333333333333,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Robert Walton\",\n        \"the lieutenant\",\n        \"the master\",\n        \"the Russian lady\",\n        \"the rival lover\",\n        \"the stranger\",\n        \"the gigantic figure in the sledge\",\n        \"the dæmon\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter begins with Robert Walton preparing for a polar expedition in Archangel, Russia, having hired a ship and assembled a crew including a noble but uneducated English lieutenant and a gentle master, while reflecting on his solitary upbringing, self-education, and longing for a sympathetic friend to temper his idealism; his journey is driven by both scientific ambition and a mystical fascination with the unknown, underscored by the lieutenant’s story of sacrificing his fortune and fiancée to a rival lover. The ship later becomes trapped in thick ice near the North Pole, where the crew witnesses a gigantic, man-like figure traveling north in a dog-drawn sledge, followed by the discovery of a second drifting sledge carrying one surviving dog and a nearly dead European stranger, emaciated and frozen, who is brought aboard and slowly revived with brandy and warmth. The stranger, speaking English with a foreign accent, reveals he was pursuing another figure—whom he calls a 'dæmon'—across the ice, and upon learning of Walton’s expedition, reacts with intense emotion, issuing a desperate warning that his own pursuit of knowledge led to ruin, and begs to tell his tragic tale, which Walton resolves to record in meticulous detail as he is captivated by the stranger’s noble demeanor, haunting voice, and transcendent appreciation for nature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 2,\n      \"start_position\": 31434,\n      \"end_position\": 41575,\n      \"word_count\": 1766,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 11.773333333333333,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Narrator (Victor)\",\n        \"Father\",\n        \"Beaufort\",\n        \"Caroline Beaufort (later mother)\",\n        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n        \"Peasant woman\",\n        \"Peasant man\",\n        \"Village priest\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter recounts the noble lineage of the narrator’s family in Geneva, detailing how his father, a respected public servant, befriended the fallen merchant Beaufort and tirelessly sought him out after his retreat into poverty in Lucerne. Upon finding Beaufort dying in a mean street near the Reuss, the father witnessed the devotion of Beaufort’s daughter Caroline, who survived through manual labor like plaiting straw and sewing, until her father’s death left her destitute. Moved by her virtue and suffering, the father took her to Geneva, protected her, and two years later married her, treating her with reverential tenderness as a woman who had endured great hardship. During their travels through Italy, Germany, and France, the narrator was born in Naples and became the center of their affection; later, while visiting the Lake of Como, his mother discovered a fair-haired orphan girl, Elizabeth Lavenza, being cared for by impoverished peasants, and after securing permission from the village priest, brought her home as a foster daughter—naming her the narrator’s 'more than sister,' a bond he embraced with childish solemnity as a divine gift.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 3,\n      \"start_position\": 41575,\n      \"end_position\": 54263,\n      \"word_count\": 2206,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 14.706666666666667,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein (narrator)\",\n        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\",\n        \"Victor's father\",\n        \"A natural philosopher (unnamed)\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter reflects on the narrator's idyllic childhood in Geneva and Belrive, where he grew up alongside Elizabeth, whose gentle nature softened his intense passions, and Henry Clerval, whose romantic imagination complemented his own intellectual fervor. At thirteen, he discovers Cornelius Agrippa’s works during a storm-bound day at an inn near Thonon, becoming obsessed with alchemy and the secrets of nature, despite his father’s dismissive remark. His devotion to these outdated theories deepens as he studies Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus, driven by a desire to unlock life’s deepest mysteries, particularly the elixir of life. At fifteen, a violent thunderstorm destroys an oak tree near their home, and a natural philosopher’s explanation of electricity and galvanism shatters his faith in the alchemists, causing him to abandon his studies in disgust and turn instead to mathematics. Though this shift brings temporary peace, the narrator believes it was a futile attempt by fate to avert the inevitable destruction that would follow his obsession with natural philosophy.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 4,\n      \"start_position\": 54263,\n      \"end_position\": 69512,\n      \"word_count\": 2675,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 17.833333333333332,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Narrator\",\n        \"Elizabeth\",\n        \"Father\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\",\n        \"M. Krempe\",\n        \"M. Waldman\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter begins with the narrator, still grieving his mother's recent death, preparing to depart Geneva for the University of Ingolstadt by carriage, accompanied by his father, friend Henry Clerval, and Elizabeth, who has recovered from scarlet fever. At Ingolstadt, he is introduced to Professor M. Krempe, whose harsh dismissal of the narrator's childhood studies in alchemy leaves him unmoved but unimpressed by the professor's crude demeanor. Later, drawn by curiosity, he attends a lecture by Professor M. Waldman, whose eloquent praise of modern chemistry—highlighting its real achievements in understanding nature—ignites a profound transformation in the narrator, awakening an obsessive ambition to surpass existing scientific boundaries. That night, unable to sleep, he resolves to abandon his alchemical pursuits and dedicate himself entirely to chemistry, leading him to seek Waldman’s guidance the next day, where he receives encouragement, book recommendations, and access to the professor’s laboratory, thus setting the course for his future destiny.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 5,\n      \"start_position\": 69512,\n      \"end_position\": 83926,\n      \"word_count\": 2535,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 16.9,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n        \"Professor Krempe\",\n        \"Professor Waldman\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter details Victor Frankenstein's obsessive two-year pursuit of natural philosophy and chemistry at the University of Ingolstadt, where he is mentored by the kind Professor Waldman and the blunt Professor Krempe. Driven by a fascination with the principle of life, Victor shifts his focus to anatomy and the decay of the human body, spending nights in charnel-houses and dissecting rooms to collect materials. He becomes consumed by the goal of bestowing life upon lifeless matter, ultimately constructing a gigantic eight-foot-tall creature in a secluded, filthy workshop at the top of his house, neglecting his health, nature, and correspondence with his family. His physical and mental state deteriorates as he endures fever, paranoia, and isolation, yet his relentless ambition sustains him until his creation nears completion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 6,\n      \"start_position\": 83926,\n      \"end_position\": 96876,\n      \"word_count\": 2357,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 15.713333333333333,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with Victor Frankenstein in the aftermath of creating the creature, overwhelmed by horror and disgust at its appearance during a dreary November night in Ingolstadt. He flees his laboratory, spending the night in agitation in the courtyard, tormented by nightmares and the terrifying sight of the creature looming over him in his bedroom. At dawn, he wanders the rain-soaked streets in a state of psychological collapse, haunted by the creature’s presence, until he is unexpectedly reunited with his friend Henry Clerval, who arrives by Swiss diligence. Victor’s emotional relief is short-lived as he fears the creature may still be in his apartment; upon returning, he finds it gone, triggering a manic outburst of joy and hysteria that culminates in a nervous breakdown and collapse. He falls into a severe fever, during which Clerval becomes his sole nurse, concealing the severity of his illness from Victor’s family and gradually nursing him back to health over several months as spring returns.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 7,\n      \"start_position\": 96876,\n      \"end_position\": 112359,\n      \"word_count\": 2718,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 18.12,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\",\n        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n        \"M. Waldman\",\n        \"M. Krempe\",\n        \"Justine Moritz\",\n        \"Ernest Frankenstein\",\n        \"William Frankenstein\",\n        \"Madame Moritz\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with Victor reading a heartfelt letter from Elizabeth, who expresses deep concern for his health and recounts domestic updates from Geneva, including Justine Moritz’s return after her mother’s death, Ernest’s aversion to study, William’s charming antics, and local gossip. Victor, moved and fatigued, writes a reply to reassure his family, marking a step in his physical recovery. He then accompanies Clerval in introducing himself to the university professors, where he endures intense emotional distress at the mention of natural philosophy—triggered by memories of his creation—though Clerval tactfully deflects the conversation. M. Krempe’s blunt praise exacerbates Victor’s anguish, while M. Waldman’s kinder approach is equally tormenting. Victor finds solace in studying Oriental languages with Clerval, whose companionship restores his connection to nature and joy, culminating in a joyful pedestrian tour of Ingolstadt’s countryside during early May, where he rediscovers happiness amid blooming flowers and cheerful villagers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 8,\n      \"start_position\": 112359,\n      \"end_position\": 132155,\n      \"word_count\": 3560,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 23.733333333333334,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n        \"Clerval\",\n        \"William Frankenstein\",\n        \"Elizabeth\",\n        \"Alphonse Frankenstein\",\n        \"Ernest Frankenstein\",\n        \"Justine Moritz\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"After receiving a devastating letter from his father detailing the murder of his young brother William in Plainpalais, Victor Frankenstein departs immediately for Geneva by cabriolet, accompanied by Clerval, who offers heartfelt but inadequate consolation. Overcome with dread as he nears his homeland, Victor pauses in Lausanne, where the serene lake and mountains briefly soothe him before grief returns. On the night he arrives, unable to enter Geneva due to closed gates, he crosses the lake by boat to visit the murder site at Plainpalais; during a violent thunderstorm, lightning flashes reveal the monstrous creature he created standing near the trees, and Victor instantly concludes it is William’s murderer. Haunted by this revelation and fearing he will be deemed insane if he speaks, he resolves to remain silent and enters his father’s house on foot at dawn, where he is overwhelmed by grief upon seeing portraits of his mother and William. His brother Ernest informs him that Justine Moritz has been accused of the crime after William’s mother’s miniature portrait was found in her possession; Victor insists Justine is innocent and knows the true murderer is the creature, but hesitates to reveal the truth. His father and Elizabeth arrive, both mourning William and fervently defending Justine’s innocence, prompting Victor to vow to prove her innocence despite the impossibility of exposing the creature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 9,\n      \"start_position\": 132155,\n      \"end_position\": 149392,\n      \"word_count\": 3093,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 20.62,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor\",\n        \"Justine\",\n        \"Elizabeth\",\n        \"The protagonist's father\",\n        \"The court officials\",\n        \"The confessor\",\n        \"The market-woman\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with the protagonist walking by foot with his family to the courthouse in Geneva for Justine’s trial, where circumstantial evidence—including a portrait found in her pocket—seems to condemn her for William’s murder. Justine maintains her innocence with calm dignity, explaining she spent the night in a barn searching for the child and is bewildered by the portrait’s presence, while Elizabeth delivers a heartfelt testimony affirming Justine’s character; despite this, public hatred and coerced pressure from her confessor lead to a guilty verdict, and Justine falsely confesses under threats of excommunication and hellfire. Later, in the dimly lit prison cell, the protagonist and Elizabeth visit Justine, who, though resigned, expresses gratitude for their belief in her innocence and reveals the confession was a lie, bidding Elizabeth a tearful farewell with blessings; the protagonist, consumed by guilt as the true murderer, suppresses his urge to confess, knowing it would not save her, and is overwhelmed by the realization that his creation has doomed William, Justine, and foresees further suffering for his father and Elizabeth.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 10,\n      \"start_position\": 149392,\n      \"end_position\": 161810,\n      \"word_count\": 2212,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"07185c06-f7d4-4b27-99ad-ef3e65820d0e","uuid":"348d24d7-3612-4c7e-b00b-966ef9274779","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"title\": \"Contents\",\n    \"author\": \"Mary Wollstonecraft (Godwin) Shelley\",\n    \"source_file\": \"Test_Texts/Frankenstein_ebook.txt\",\n    \"source_format\": \"txt\",\n    \"total_word_count\": 75060,\n    \"total_character_count\": 418299,\n    \"estimated_total_duration_minutes\": 499.2266666666667,\n    \"words_per_minute\": 150\n  },\n  \"structure\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 1,\n      \"start_position\": 1000,\n      \"end_position\": 31434,\n      \"word_count\": 5441,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 36.27333333333333,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Robert Walton\",\n        \"the lieutenant\",\n        \"the master\",\n        \"the Russian lady\",\n        \"the rival lover\",\n        \"the stranger\",\n        \"the gigantic figure in the sledge\",\n        \"the dæmon\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter begins with Robert Walton preparing for a polar expedition in Archangel, Russia, having hired a ship and assembled a crew including a noble but uneducated English lieutenant and a gentle master, while reflecting on his solitary upbringing, self-education, and longing for a sympathetic friend to temper his idealism; his journey is driven by both scientific ambition and a mystical fascination with the unknown, underscored by the lieutenant’s story of sacrificing his fortune and fiancée to a rival lover. The ship later becomes trapped in thick ice near the North Pole, where the crew witnesses a gigantic, man-like figure traveling north in a dog-drawn sledge, followed by the discovery of a second drifting sledge carrying one surviving dog and a nearly dead European stranger, emaciated and frozen, who is brought aboard and slowly revived with brandy and warmth. The stranger, speaking English with a foreign accent, reveals he was pursuing another figure—whom he calls a 'dæmon'—across the ice, and upon learning of Walton’s expedition, reacts with intense emotion, issuing a desperate warning that his own pursuit of knowledge led to ruin, and begs to tell his tragic tale, which Walton resolves to record in meticulous detail as he is captivated by the stranger’s noble demeanor, haunting voice, and transcendent appreciation for nature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 2,\n      \"start_position\": 31434,\n      \"end_position\": 41575,\n      \"word_count\": 1766,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 11.773333333333333,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Narrator (Victor)\",\n        \"Father\",\n        \"Beaufort\",\n        \"Caroline Beaufort (later mother)\",\n        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n        \"Peasant woman\",\n        \"Peasant man\",\n        \"Village priest\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter recounts the noble lineage of the narrator’s family in Geneva, detailing how his father, a respected public servant, befriended the fallen merchant Beaufort and tirelessly sought him out after his retreat into poverty in Lucerne. Upon finding Beaufort dying in a mean street near the Reuss, the father witnessed the devotion of Beaufort’s daughter Caroline, who survived through manual labor like plaiting straw and sewing, until her father’s death left her destitute. Moved by her virtue and suffering, the father took her to Geneva, protected her, and two years later married her, treating her with reverential tenderness as a woman who had endured great hardship. During their travels through Italy, Germany, and France, the narrator was born in Naples and became the center of their affection; later, while visiting the Lake of Como, his mother discovered a fair-haired orphan girl, Elizabeth Lavenza, being cared for by impoverished peasants, and after securing permission from the village priest, brought her home as a foster daughter—naming her the narrator’s 'more than sister,' a bond he embraced with childish solemnity as a divine gift.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 3,\n      \"start_position\": 41575,\n      \"end_position\": 54263,\n      \"word_count\": 2206,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 14.706666666666667,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein (narrator)\",\n        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\",\n        \"Victor's father\",\n        \"A natural philosopher (unnamed)\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter reflects on the narrator's idyllic childhood in Geneva and Belrive, where he grew up alongside Elizabeth, whose gentle nature softened his intense passions, and Henry Clerval, whose romantic imagination complemented his own intellectual fervor. At thirteen, he discovers Cornelius Agrippa’s works during a storm-bound day at an inn near Thonon, becoming obsessed with alchemy and the secrets of nature, despite his father’s dismissive remark. His devotion to these outdated theories deepens as he studies Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus, driven by a desire to unlock life’s deepest mysteries, particularly the elixir of life. At fifteen, a violent thunderstorm destroys an oak tree near their home, and a natural philosopher’s explanation of electricity and galvanism shatters his faith in the alchemists, causing him to abandon his studies in disgust and turn instead to mathematics. Though this shift brings temporary peace, the narrator believes it was a futile attempt by fate to avert the inevitable destruction that would follow his obsession with natural philosophy.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 4,\n      \"start_position\": 54263,\n      \"end_position\": 69512,\n      \"word_count\": 2675,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 17.833333333333332,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Narrator\",\n        \"Elizabeth\",\n        \"Father\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\",\n        \"M. Krempe\",\n        \"M. Waldman\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter begins with the narrator, still grieving his mother's recent death, preparing to depart Geneva for the University of Ingolstadt by carriage, accompanied by his father, friend Henry Clerval, and Elizabeth, who has recovered from scarlet fever. At Ingolstadt, he is introduced to Professor M. Krempe, whose harsh dismissal of the narrator's childhood studies in alchemy leaves him unmoved but unimpressed by the professor's crude demeanor. Later, drawn by curiosity, he attends a lecture by Professor M. Waldman, whose eloquent praise of modern chemistry—highlighting its real achievements in understanding nature—ignites a profound transformation in the narrator, awakening an obsessive ambition to surpass existing scientific boundaries. That night, unable to sleep, he resolves to abandon his alchemical pursuits and dedicate himself entirely to chemistry, leading him to seek Waldman’s guidance the next day, where he receives encouragement, book recommendations, and access to the professor’s laboratory, thus setting the course for his future destiny.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 5,\n      \"start_position\": 69512,\n      \"end_position\": 83926,\n      \"word_count\": 2535,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 16.9,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n        \"Professor Krempe\",\n        \"Professor Waldman\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter details Victor Frankenstein's obsessive two-year pursuit of natural philosophy and chemistry at the University of Ingolstadt, where he is mentored by the kind Professor Waldman and the blunt Professor Krempe. Driven by a fascination with the principle of life, Victor shifts his focus to anatomy and the decay of the human body, spending nights in charnel-houses and dissecting rooms to collect materials. He becomes consumed by the goal of bestowing life upon lifeless matter, ultimately constructing a gigantic eight-foot-tall creature in a secluded, filthy workshop at the top of his house, neglecting his health, nature, and correspondence with his family. His physical and mental state deteriorates as he endures fever, paranoia, and isolation, yet his relentless ambition sustains him until his creation nears completion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 6,\n      \"start_position\": 83926,\n      \"end_position\": 96876,\n      \"word_count\": 2357,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 15.713333333333333,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with Victor Frankenstein in the aftermath of creating the creature, overwhelmed by horror and disgust at its appearance during a dreary November night in Ingolstadt. He flees his laboratory, spending the night in agitation in the courtyard, tormented by nightmares and the terrifying sight of the creature looming over him in his bedroom. At dawn, he wanders the rain-soaked streets in a state of psychological collapse, haunted by the creature’s presence, until he is unexpectedly reunited with his friend Henry Clerval, who arrives by Swiss diligence. Victor’s emotional relief is short-lived as he fears the creature may still be in his apartment; upon returning, he finds it gone, triggering a manic outburst of joy and hysteria that culminates in a nervous breakdown and collapse. He falls into a severe fever, during which Clerval becomes his sole nurse, concealing the severity of his illness from Victor’s family and gradually nursing him back to health over several months as spring returns.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 7,\n      \"start_position\": 96876,\n      \"end_position\": 112359,\n      \"word_count\": 2718,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 18.12,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\",\n        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n        \"M. Waldman\",\n        \"M. Krempe\",\n        \"Justine Moritz\",\n        \"Ernest Frankenstein\",\n        \"William Frankenstein\",\n        \"Madame Moritz\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with Victor reading a heartfelt letter from Elizabeth, who expresses deep concern for his health and recounts domestic updates from Geneva, including Justine Moritz’s return after her mother’s death, Ernest’s aversion to study, William’s charming antics, and local gossip. Victor, moved and fatigued, writes a reply to reassure his family, marking a step in his physical recovery. He then accompanies Clerval in introducing himself to the university professors, where he endures intense emotional distress at the mention of natural philosophy—triggered by memories of his creation—though Clerval tactfully deflects the conversation. M. Krempe’s blunt praise exacerbates Victor’s anguish, while M. Waldman’s kinder approach is equally tormenting. Victor finds solace in studying Oriental languages with Clerval, whose companionship restores his connection to nature and joy, culminating in a joyful pedestrian tour of Ingolstadt’s countryside during early May, where he rediscovers happiness amid blooming flowers and cheerful villagers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 8,\n      \"start_position\": 112359,\n      \"end_position\": 132155,\n      \"word_count\": 3560,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 23.733333333333334,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein\",\n        \"Clerval\",\n        \"William Frankenstein\",\n        \"Elizabeth\",\n        \"Alphonse Frankenstein\",\n        \"Ernest Frankenstein\",\n        \"Justine Moritz\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"After receiving a devastating letter from his father detailing the murder of his young brother William in Plainpalais, Victor Frankenstein departs immediately for Geneva by cabriolet, accompanied by Clerval, who offers heartfelt but inadequate consolation. Overcome with dread as he nears his homeland, Victor pauses in Lausanne, where the serene lake and mountains briefly soothe him before grief returns. On the night he arrives, unable to enter Geneva due to closed gates, he crosses the lake by boat to visit the murder site at Plainpalais; during a violent thunderstorm, lightning flashes reveal the monstrous creature he created standing near the trees, and Victor instantly concludes it is William’s murderer. Haunted by this revelation and fearing he will be deemed insane if he speaks, he resolves to remain silent and enters his father’s house on foot at dawn, where he is overwhelmed by grief upon seeing portraits of his mother and William. His brother Ernest informs him that Justine Moritz has been accused of the crime after William’s mother’s miniature portrait was found in her possession; Victor insists Justine is innocent and knows the true murderer is the creature, but hesitates to reveal the truth. His father and Elizabeth arrive, both mourning William and fervently defending Justine’s innocence, prompting Victor to vow to prove her innocence despite the impossibility of exposing the creature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 9,\n      \"start_position\": 132155,\n      \"end_position\": 149392,\n      \"word_count\": 3093,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 20.62,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\