h no shared words, including valid relational descriptor merges like \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"\n   145→\n   146→**Root Cause Investigation:**\n   147→- Traced through `_validate_merge` function in `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py`\n   148→- Found that after checking various special cases, line 2001-2011 had a blanket rejection for ANY pair with zero shared words\n   149→- \"my father\" (words: \"my\", \"father\") and \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (words: \"alphonse\", \"frankenstein\") share NO words\n   150→- Since both are multi-word names, they didn't match the single-word special case logic\n   151→- The code reached the final \"no shared words = reject\" clause and was blocked\n   152→\n   153→**Fix Applied:**\n   154→- Modified `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` lines 2001-2042\n   155→- Added special case handling BEFORE the blanket rejection for relational/descriptive terms\n   156→- Detects if either name contains relational terms (father, mother, uncle, etc.) or descriptive patterns (creature, monster, man, etc.)\n   157→- For relational/descriptive pairs with chapter overlap, allow merge with confidence based on overlap ratio:\n   158→  - overlap_ratio > 0.3: confidence 0.75\n   159→  - any overlap: confidence 0.65\n   160→  - no overlap: reject with confidence 0.3\n   161→- This allows \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" to merge if they appear in overlapping chapters\n   162→\n   163→**Testing:** All 444 tests pass.\n   164→\n   165→**Expected Impact:** Should significantly improve character alias resolution for Frankenstein, reducing character fragmentation.\n   166→\n   167→**Result:** FAILED\n   168→- ✅ \"the creature\" still correctly merged with \"the monster\" and \"the wretch\" (maintained from Attempt 2)\n   169→- ❌ \"my father\" (53), \"Father\" (6), \"M.\" (23), \"M. Frankenstein\" (2), \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (1) STILL separate\n   170→- ❌ \"Walton\" (6) and \"Captain Walton\" (1) STILL separat